's get into the review and and see  how it does  by the way it runs a little warm which  tells me that it uses fair bit of power  i didn't try to measure it but  it may not last long on a phone that's  not plugged in  uh on this thing um we always start with  our dashboard but since we have two  outputs balanced and unbalanced  we have got to test them both  independently so this is with 3.5  millimeter  output which puts out the same 2.1 volts  that the  previous generation did and it's the  same voltage that desktop tax  produced which is quite good but when  you go to unbalance the voltage jumps up  to 4 volts  which is excellent 4.2 volts almost and  uh  look at the distortion here the highest  peak is third harmonic at minus 125 db  remember i said we are what we're  looking for is first as far as threshold  here is -115  and this thing's 10 db better than  threshold of hearing and  you're getting in this tiny little  package and  uh noise though adds to that and  drags sign that down to 112 and when i  said drag  it's still incredibly good look at where  it lands  this area is really top of the class the  ones in blue  and this thing is in the middle there's  probably i'd say 200 decks that are  worse than it  some of them probably ten or twelve  thousand dollar decks that can't do as  well as this little  uh tiny dongle can do um  signal noise ratio is great uh not state  of the art by desktop standards but by  dongles it's great you're getting  19 and a half bits of dynamic range  at full volume when you crank it down to  50 millivolts which is what we use to  test for hissed noise it drops down to  80 db  for a desktop deck that wouldn't be very  good it basically says if you play as  louder than 80 db you're going to hear  potentially some background hiss  in there with sensitive iem so uh it  does the job but if you have super  sensitive iem this may not be as good  it also possible that this noise is  dependent on what you plug it into  these little devices don't have a lot of  filtering in them so  you know my desktop windows machine that  i use to test with  generates a lot of noise over usb so  it's possible that might be a little  cleaner on  on some other device that you plug it  into  uh jitter noise in these little dongles  again for the same reason  their jitter performance or gender tests  that we run  always shows a lot of you know spikes  and noise from the  host that bleeds into them again there's  not enough room in there to put  lots of regulation and filtering in  there but still look at the  levels of them the worst case is  something at two kilohertz is minus 120  db  so well below threshold of hearing and  all these other ones are clearly well  below the threshold of hearing so very  very controlled  even though it looks dirty to the eye  part of the reason looks bad is because  the noise floor is so low  a lot of these dongles hover up here uh  if you look at the typical google  dongles and what have you the noise  floors up here which  tends to hide a lot of this because this  thing is so clean  on its own it shows some of the usb  activity  that's bleeding up here a lot of people  complain that why do you test with just  one tone so all right we'll test with 32  tones and so these are 32 tones and  these tones if the device is not 100  linear  meaning transparent then they generate  intermodulation distortions and they all  pile up in what we call grass down here  uh we see that at low frequencies there  is no grass to speak of  uh we're at 120 db which is 20 bits of  distortion free range and here it even  goes down as we go up in frequencies  and we get up to 21 bits this is  beyond good uh i mean this is desktop  class best measure performance ever i  have is 22 bits  for state-of-the-art dac and here we  have a little dongle  costing 100 bucks that's getting up to  21 bits so  distortion is just not in the equation  this thing is going to  sound clean clean clean  there's a weird thing in that my audio  analyzer only talks through this  interface called asio and the azio  interface that i have for certain class  of devices like these dongles  the emulator layer that i have always  screws up and adds its own noise in  there  i don't know why and it's an open source  uh it's a freebie type of  plugin that does that and there's no  support for why it doesn't so  uh please excuse the fact that this is  elevated noise it's actual noise is not  up here it's probably somewhere down  here  um so but still let's look at how much  power we get  at max level we get 52 milliwatts  i'll show you in a second what a typical  dongle produces this is  a lot of power uh with a 300 ohm load  and it's not because we have four volts  of output and that's what helps there  searching to 33 ohm load where we stress  current delivery now  we get 0.1 watt which is a lot of power  again in the low dongle  how much power look at that 300 ohm  where it finishes  these other two devices are huge they're  more  powerful than their massive little  bricks  nothing this small has this kind of  power uh if you go over here  um [ __ ] dragonfly black which is you  know audioquest  dongle that a lot of people buy look at  where that is waiting down here  it doesn't even put out one mil water  power in there um  apple dongle 3.6 milliwatts and that's  one of the better ones and instead of  3.6  you have 52 milliwatts in here so you  have 15 times more power  than an apple dongle uh the s8 that was  uh before it didn't have as much power  because it was unbalanced  and so put out half the voltage and you  get quarter of the power  on this thing switching to 33 ohm load  um  we see that again it performs superbly  and  it produces 110 milliwatts  again going down here let's see if we  can find  the apple dongle there's a samsung phone  that i have my own uh samsung s8 plus  puts out 28 milliwatts this thing puts  out 110 millibars so four times more  than my phone by itself  which is quite 